In this lesson, we'll look at expanding and factorising, two sides of the same coin. Both rely on the distributive law, which is just a way of saying multiplication and addition play nicely together. First, let's talk about expanding. When you see something like a times a bracket, you simply multiply that outside term by every single thing inside. So if the bracket has two terms, you get two products, and if it has three terms, you get three products. It works the same way with subtraction, because you're really just adding a negative. Next comes factorising, which is the reverse move. You look at all the terms in your expression and ask, what is the biggest thing I can pull out of every single one of them? For numbers, you take the biggest common divisor. For letters, you grab the lowest power that appears in every term. Pull that out and leave the rest tucked inside a bracket. Why bother? Expanding helps you tidy up expressions that have brackets, while factorising helps you see hidden structure and makes later algebra way easier. One quick watch out though, make sure you distribute across every term inside the bracket, never miss one. Now let's see it in action.